  "strategy": "We are to search for a sequence of non-negative coefficients (heights) of length n (where n is the number of items) that minimizes the evaluation score, defined as:\n\n    score = (2 * n * max(autocorrelation)) / (sum(asequence)) ** 2\n\nThe autocorrelation is computed via full convolution, so for a sequence `a` of length `n`, the convolution is `c = np.convolve(a, a, mode='full')` which has length `2*n-1`, and we take the maximum value from this convolution.\n\nOur approach:\n1. Initial sequence generation using provided information or a random structured method.\n2. Primary optimization with gradient-based methods, particularly with the Adam algorithm adapted for large dimensions.\n3. Post-optimization: Coordinate descent and peak shaving.\n4. If time remains, secondary exploration on other sequence lengths.\n\nSteps:\n    1. Use the provided_sequence if available to initialize the candidate.\n    2. Normalize it to the desired norm (if not already of length 256).\n    3. Use the initial candidate to start the primary optimization which uses gradient descent.\n    4. During primary optimization, we periodically check the score and update the best candidate.\n    5. We also include an exact subgradient method in the final phase to escape local minima.\n    6. After primary, we do a peak shaving and coordinate descent polish.\n    7. Use $2 \\times 16 \\ \\text{seconds}$ for secondary explorations if time allows.\n\nWe will structure the code to transparently follow the parent's format and use the exact_score function from the parent.\n\nBut note: since we are to return a complete candidate with all numbers explicitly nonnegative and at most 1000, we must ensure that our search respects these bounds.\n\nWe then apply a final exact_normalization and evaluate the score with the full-convolution method.\n\nWe set a global time budget of 2400 seconds for the entire run.\n\nLet's start with the provided sequence (if available) and then proceed.",
